<header><p class="enc-kicker">Place · mountain</p><h1 class="enc-title">Pamir Mountains</h1><p class="enc-meta">Also known as Pamirs</p></header>
<div class="enc-body"><p class="enc-desc">The Pamir Mountains are a high mountain system of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/central-asia">Central Asia</a>, lying mainly in Tajikistan and reaching into <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/afghanistan">Afghanistan</a>, <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/china">China</a> and Kyrgyzstan, with peaks above 7,000 metres. Known as the 'Roof of the World', they were crossed by branches of the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/silk-road">Silk Road</a> linking the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/tarim-basin">Tarim Basin</a> with <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/bactria">Bactria</a> and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/india">India</a>. Ancient Saka groups lived in and around them.</p><figure class="enc-fig"><img src="/images/encyclopedia/pamir-mountains.jpg" alt="Pamir Mountains" width="320" height="240" fetchpriority="high"><figcaption>Image: Caroline Gutman · CC0 · <a href="https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Gutman_Karakul_lake.jpg" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Wikimedia Commons</a></figcaption></figure></div>
